@page
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About";
}
<article class="prose">
  <h1>About Huddle Board</h1>
  <p class="lede">Less explaining. More playing. Huddle Board helps a coach show young
    flag football players where to line up and what to do. The whole app is free,
    requires no account, and works offline.</p>
  <p>It started on a real sideline, coaching 8U flag football. The coaches knew
    football; the hard part was explaining it to six seven-year-olds in the twenty
    seconds between plays. So the tool does not simplify the football. It simplifies the
    explaining.</p>

  <h2>The whole app is free</h2>
  <p>The play library, drawing tools, animation, individual player steps and printing
    are all included. No subscription, paid upgrade or payment details. Open it,
    use it with your team, and pass it on to next season's coach.</p>
  <p>Huddle Board is also open source under the MIT license. You can keep a copy,
    adapt it for your team, or share it with your league. The source and its history
    are on <a href="https://github.com/crawfordjustin/huddle-board">GitHub</a>.</p>

  <h2>Built for the coach on the field</h2>
  <p>Huddle Board is built for 6U to 8U flag football, or any division where the coach
    is on the field with the kids. Choose 5-on-5, 6-on-6 or 7-on-7: each has its own
    formations, play library and starting deck. Switching formats keeps the choices
    you made in each one.</p>
  <p>Built for an 11-inch tablet, Huddle Board runs in the browser and installs to the
    home screen. Open it with a connection to get it ready, then use it with no signal
    at the field. See <a href="/#install">how to install it</a>.</p>

  <h2>Familiar words. Clear jobs.</h2>
  <p>Sides are BLUE and ORANGE, so the words stay the same whether you're beside
    the kids or facing them. Each player has a shape, a color and a spoken name
    instead of a position abbreviation to decode.</p>
  <p>The teaching vocabulary centers on nine route shapes: GO, OUT, IN, SIT,
    CORNER, POST, WHEEL, SWING and CARRY. New plays reuse familiar jobs, so you can
    build on what the team already knows.</p>
  <p>Those choices came from practice. Letters on the field were tried with real
    kids and did not land. The design keeps the football and makes the explaining
    easier.</p>

  <h2>Show the whole play, then teach one kid</h2>
  <p>A play opens on its finished picture: every route drawn, every job in the call
    strip, and the ball where it ends up. <b>Line up</b> returns everyone to their
    spots. <b>Run it</b> plays once from the snap and holds the finish; <b>Replay</b>
    shows it again. The ball follows the real exchanges, including handoffs, reverses
    and throws.</p>
  <p>Tap a player to walk through that kid's job with <b>Next</b>, <b>Back</b> and
    <b>Again</b>. Only that player moves, so the steps are easy to follow. Tap
    <b>All</b> to return to the whole play. <b>Mirror</b> swaps the players and routes
    to the other side while the field stays put.</p>
  <p>Use the optional <b>3D</b> view to show the field from behind the offense.
    Animation and individual steps work in either view.</p>

  <h2>Build the deck your team needs</h2>
  <p>Use <b>Change plays</b> in the menu to add or remove plays. Search the library,
    narrow it by run, pass or situation, and tap a thumbnail for a larger preview.
    The deck has the same filters for finding a call between plays.</p>
  <p>Week 1 starts with four calls: a run, a quick pass, a third-down play and a
    goal-line play. Weeks 2–6 start empty. Save your deck into a week and give the
    week your own name; taking a week replaces the deck after two taps.</p>
  <p>Choose the real play names or the fun names the kids shout, or rename either
    yourself. Search still finds the original names. Hide shipped plays you will
    never call from <b>Setup → Your own → Shipped plays</b>; you can show them again
    whenever you need them.</p>
  <p>Add your own coaching notes from a play's <b>Notes</b> button. In the library,
    <b>Print</b> turns your deck into a reference sheet with your names and drawings;
    coaching notes stay off the sheet.</p>

  <h2>Draw your own plays</h2>
  <p>Tap <b>New play</b> in the library to move players, draw routes and set who gets
    the ball. Build runs, passes, reverses, rollouts and passes thrown by a back.
    Preview the animation before saving. The designer checks the drawing for errors
    using the same rules as the shipped library.</p>
  <p>You can also start from an existing play: open its library preview and choose
    <b>Copy to edit</b>. Save the result as your own play, keeping the original.
    Copies carry the routes and ball assignments; some details, such as fake
    handoffs and route delays, do not carry over.</p>
  <p>Saved drawings join your deck and have the same animation and step-by-step jobs
    as shipped plays. They stay with the format you drew them for and survive app
    updates and <b>Start over</b>.</p>

  <h2>Keep the game moving</h2>
  <p>The play clock starts with a tap. In <b>Setup → Clock</b>, choose its length,
    warning time and vibration, or turn it off. A first-start tutorial walks through
    the app; reopen it any time from the menu.</p>
  <p>The tablet keeps a game log of plays shown and clock starts, which you can
    export or clear in <b>Setup → Tablet</b>. When an app update is ready, the offer
    appears on the intro or deck and waits for your tap.</p>

  <h2>Your setup stays with you</h2>
  <p>Your decks, names, notes, weeks, hidden plays, drawings and settings are kept on the
    tablet through updates. There is no account or automatic cloud sync.</p>
  <p>To copy the setup to an assistant coach's tablet or keep a backup, use
    <b>Setup → Tablet → Sync tablets</b> to export a file. Share it or download it,
    then import it on the other tablet. Import replaces that tablet's setup across
    all three formats, including drawn plays; export its setup first if you want
    to keep it. The game log stays on its own tablet and is exported separately.</p>

  <p><a class="go big" href="/app/">Open Huddle Board — it's free</a></p>
</article>
